<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles-Denis Bourbaki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1816">1816</a>–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles-Denis Bourbaki was a French general of Greek descent who commanded the Imperial Guard in 1870 and then the Army of the East in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. After he failed to relieve Belfort, his defeated army of about 87,000 men crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in February 1871, where it was disarmed and interned, an event depicted in the Bourbaki Panorama at Lucerne.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-denis-bourbaki.jpg" alt="Charles-Denis Bourbaki" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pierre-Louis Pierson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_Bourbaki.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bourbaki was born at Pau on 22 April 1816, the son of the Greek colonel Constantin Denis Bourbaki, who died in the Greek War of Independence in 1827. He was educated at the Prytanée National Militaire and at Saint-Cyr, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zouaves">Zouaves</a> in 1836, became a lieutenant of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-foreign-legion">Foreign Legion</a> in 1838 and served as aide-de-camp to King Louis Philippe.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He rose in the campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>: captain in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zouaves">Zouaves</a> in 1842, colonel of the Turcos in 1847 and brigadier-general in 1854. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> he commanded part of the Algerian troops at the Alma, Inkerman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a>. Made general of division in 1857, he took command at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1859 and did well in the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, second only to MacMahon. In 1862 he was proposed for the vacant Greek throne but declined it.</p><p>Commanding the Imperial Guard around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> in 1870, he was criticised for refusing to commit its reserves at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gravelotte">Battle of Gravelotte</a>. During the siege of Metz a businessman, Edmond Régnier, told Marshal Bazaine on the empress's alleged authority that peace was near and that Bourbaki or Marshal Canrobert should go to Hastings. Bourbaki went, with Prussian connivance, only to learn from the empress Eugénie that he had been tricked, and he was then refused re-entry to Metz on a technicality.</p><p>He offered his services to Léon Gambetta and, after a short command of the Northern Army, led the Army of the East. Its attempt on Belfort began with victory at the Battle of Villersexel and ended in defeat in the three-day battle of the Lisaine. Pressed by Field Marshal Edwin Freiherr von Manteuffel, short of food and reduced from 150,000 men, the army crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> at Les Verrières, Sainte-Croix, Vallorbe and the Vallée de Joux, and was held for six weeks before being repatriated in March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 26 January 1871 Bourbaki handed his command to General Justin Clinchant and, rather than face surrender, shot himself in the forehead that night; he survived, was carried into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and recovered. In July 1871 he again took command at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, later becoming its military governor. He was retired in 1881 for his political opinions, failed to win a seat in the Senate in 1885, and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a> on 22 September 1897. The Bourbaki group of 20th-century French mathematicians took his surname as their collective pseudonym.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles-Denis Bourbaki” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In February 1871 about 87,000 men of the defeated French Army of the East under General <b>Bourbaki</b> were disarmed and interned.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Swiss Confederation · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pau, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonne">Bayonne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.3,-0.37&amp;year=1868" aria-label="Pau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Charles-Denis Bourbaki"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4977 3873h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4977 3873h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.3,-0.37&amp;year=1868">What was here in 1868? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
